spans.s:
kṣema, (1, as in Skt., adj. causing peace and comfort, or n. comfort: Mv 〔i.302.1〕 kiṃ kṣemaṃ, what (can we do that) will cause you peace and comfort? In the verse form 〔303.21〕 replaced by kiṃ karoma. Response to a cry of distress; foll. by kiṃ avidhāvidhaṃ ti krandasi. Senart fails to understand.)
(2) n. of a king of old (= Pali Khema, oftener Khemaṃkara, q.v. DPPN): Divy 〔242.5〕 (his capital was Kṣemāvatī, q.v.);
(3) n. of a Buddha: Mmk 〔365.17〕 (vs, but submetrical; one syllable short).

Kṣemā,
(1) (= Pali Khemā) n. of Śākyamuniʼs chief female disciple: Mv 〔i.251.21〕 (agrā … śrāvikā);
(2) n. of a daughter of King Prasenajit, who also became a nun in the Buddhaʼs order: Av 〔ii.46.7 ff.〕; in 〔50.9〕 she is, in fact, declared by Buddha to be agrā me … bhikṣuṇīnāṃ mama śrāvikāṇāṃ, so that she is actually identified with (1); her story, however (Av 〔no. 79〕) is wholly different from the story of Khemā in Pali. (Mv gives no story about Kṣemā 1.).

Kṣemaṃkara,
(1) n. of a former Buddha: Divy 〔242.1 ff.〕; he lived in Kṣemāvatī; in the same story in Pali Khemaṃkara is the n. of the king of the city, who in Divy (〔242.5〕) is Kṣema (in Pali also alternatively Khema); in Pali the Buddha Kakusaṃdha is born under this king (in Divy Krakucchanda appears in a sequel to the story, 〔254.3〕);
(2) n. of an apparently different former Buddha: Av 〔i.110.10 ff.〕;
(3) n. of a son of King Brahmadatta of Benares: Av 〔ii.46.6〕; 〔49.8〕;
(4) n. of a Bodhisattva: Gv 〔442.16〕;
(5) n. of a nāga: Mvy 〔3336〕.
